# KEYMAP.md §Daemon-down surfaces (C) · UX-SPEC.md §3.12 C — `Esc` dismisses the reconnect
# banner and the daemon dot stays red; its ✕ (`banner.close`) does the same by pointer.
#
# The banner is the only surface whose `Esc` hides a *problem* rather than an overlay, so the
# thing worth pinning is that dismissing it changes the banner and nothing else: the link is
# still lost, `daemon.dismissed` records the dismissal, and the Hub gets its own `Esc` back
# because `Daemon > Banner` has left the context chain.
#
# Headless: virtual-lane only, because of its `shot`. The keyboard does reach the focused view
# in the headless lane (§7), so everything else here would run there.
fixture: one-repo
await idle 20000
daemon kill
await daemon.link == lost 30000
assert key_contexts[2] == "Daemon" && key_contexts[3] == "Banner" && daemon.dismissed == false
shot banner

# `Esc` here is the banner's, not the Hub's: it dismisses, and the red dot stays red.
key escape
await daemon.dismissed == true 5000
assert daemon.link == "lost" && key_contexts[0] == "Hub" && key_contexts[1] == "Worktrees"
assert key_contexts[2] absent
dump dismissed

# The pointer path: bring fleetd back, lose it again, and dismiss the new banner with its ✕,
# which dispatches the banner's own `Esc` action (ADR 0023).
daemon restart
await daemon.link == reconnected 60000
advance 7000
await daemon.link == connected 5000
daemon kill
await daemon.link == lost 30000
assert daemon.dismissed == false && targets["banner.close"] exists
click banner.close
await daemon.dismissed == true 5000
assert daemon.link == "lost" && key_contexts[0] == "Hub" && key_contexts[2] absent
assert targets["banner.close"] absent
dump dismissed-by-pointer
quit
